1. d4 { Notes by GM Ray Keene } 1... d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 dxc4 5. a4 Bf5 6. Ne5 e6 7. f3 c5 { This is unusual. More common is 7
... Bb4 which can lead to great complications after 8 e4
Bxe4. } 8. e4 Bg6 { Again Black has a more complex alternative
available with 8 ... cxd4 but Kramnik is determined to play
quietly. } 9. Be3 cxd4 10. Qxd4 Qxd4 11. Bxd4 Nfd7 12. Nxd7 Nxd7 13. Bxc4 a6 14. Ke2 Rg8 { This is necessary in order to develop
his f8-bishop. White is a long way ahead in development and,
optically at least, his position looks promising. However,
Topalov proves unable to convert this into any more concrete. } 15. Rhd1 Rc8 16. b3 Bc5 17. a5 Ke7 18. Na4 Bb4 19. Nb6 Nxb6 20. Bxb6 f6 21. Rd3 Rc6 22. h4 Rgc8 23. g4 { Topalov's kingside advances
look attractive but they don't really get anywhere. } 23... Bc5 24. Rad1 Bxb6 { Kramnik has seen a path to complete equality. } 25. Rd7+ Kf8 26. axb6 Rxb6 27. R1d6 Rxd6 28. Rxd6 Rc6 29. Rxc6 bxc6 30. b4 e5 31. Bxa6 1/2-1/2

